    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-13807",
      "knowledge_point_id": "41b7d83f-3b9b-556c-9e49-13df9c3b597e",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating Functions Containing the Natural Logarithm",
      "problem": "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x=\\frac{2}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}\\cdot  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{u}^{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
