    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-351287",
      "knowledge_point_id": "37a7aff6-d1b2-5383-bb26-aa6363594b05",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating Definite Integrals",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{5}^{125}\\frac{8}{3x{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $u={log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot 2x=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $125$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $6$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{125}\\frac{8}{3x{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{125}\\frac{1}{{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2})}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{4}{3}\\ln(3)"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "\\frac{4}{3}\\ln(3)",
          "choices_complete": false,
          "correct_origin": "ma_successful_grade"
        }
      ],
