    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-346434",
      "knowledge_point_id": "37a7aff6-d1b2-5383-bb26-aa6363594b05",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating Definite Integrals",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e$ | ${e}^{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{4}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot {u}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}({4}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =\\frac{75}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{75}{8}"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "\\frac{75}{8}",
          "choices_complete": false,
          "correct_origin": "ma_successful_grade"
        }
      ],
